## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design branchless bit tricks for palindrome detection on 64-bit values, a common bitboard problem.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR-based bit reversal
- Branchless boolean results
- Fixed-width masking patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverse64(n) {
  n = ((n >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  n = ((n >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  n = ((n >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  n = ((n >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  n = (n >> 32n) | (n << 32n);
  return n;
}
function isPalindrome64(n) {
  return reverse64(n) === n;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Targeted to assess bit‑manipulation proficiency across multi‑word integers, with emphasis on branchless design and fixed work per input.

## Key Concepts
- XOR for difference on multi-word values
- 64-bit popcount with masks and shifts
- Branchless accumulation across halves

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch (C-like)
int hammingDistance128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t lo = (a.lo ^ b.lo);
  uint64_t hi = (a.hi ^ b.hi);
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t countLo = (lo * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t countHi = (hi * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  return (int)(countLo + countHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to a 256-bit type?
- Compare branchless popcount against using CPU intrinsics.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit hacks and understanding of 8×8 transpose, a common optimization in graphics, crypto, and compression. It checks for precise, branchless manipulation and correct use of masks.

## Key Concepts
- fixed-sequence bit swaps
- 8×8 bit matrix representation
- mask constants, shifts, no loops

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t transpose64(uint64_t x) {
    const uint64_t m1  = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
    x = (x & m1) << 1 | (x >> 1) & m1;
    const uint64_t m2  = 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    x = (x & m2) << 2 | (x >> 2) & m2;
    const uint64_t m4  = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    x = (x & m4) << 4 | (x >> 4) & m4;
    const uint64_t m8  = 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    x = (x & m8) << 8 | (x >> 8) & m8;
    const uint64_t m16 = 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    x = (x & m16) << 16 | (x >> 16) & m16;
    const uint64_t m32 = 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
    x = (x & m32) << 32 | (x >> 32) & m32;
    return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with randomized 64-bit inputs to validate correctness?
- How would you generalize to 16×16 blocks with a 256-bit input stored in four 64-bit words?

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a branchless bit-packing operation across 128 bits—a common low-level optimization used in compression algorithms, bitmap libraries, and SIMD data processing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit masks and shifts
- Branchless algorithms
- Parallel bit extraction and compression

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 compress128(uint128 x, uint128 mask) {
    // Stage 1: compact adjacent bits (shift 1)
    uint128 m = mask & ~(mask << 1);
    x = (x & m) | ((x >> 1) & ~m);
    mask &= ~(mask << 1);

    // Subsequent stages (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) follow the same pattern
    // with appropriate shift amounts...

    return x;
}
